{"availability":"available","full":"Skitionek/route-with-dynamic-outlets","name":"route-with-dynamic-outlets","description":"Library/snippet showing how to cleanly integrate panels & tabs with angular router","category":"Developer tools","language":"TypeScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2023-07-15T08:04:39Z","updated":"2026-10-10T00:14:38Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://skitionek.github.io/route-with-dynamic-outlets/","topics":[],"demo":"https://skitionek.github.io/route-with-dynamic-outlets/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.294Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.294Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.294Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.294Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["[][build-url] [][npm-url] [][downloads-url] [][issues-url] [][codecov-url] [][semantic-release-url] [][commitizen-url] [](","Larger Angular applications often need panels or tabs interfaces where each panel is driven by the URL. Most existing implementations use a single route and manage panel state on top of it — this works for simple cases but loses all the advanced features the Angular Router offers (lazy loading, guards, nested routes, etc.).","route-with-dynamic-outlets solves this by dynamically registering named children at match time, so every panel is a first-class Angular route. The library is a focused proof of concept that shows a clean, router-native direction for advanced panel/tab UIs."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Skitionek/route-with-dynamic-outlets/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"a43c8c696dfdee9e21a64c34847e19ddf413542a547ec202d90d9bbf47aafff2","extractedAt":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.362Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-10T23:19:47.362Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"[][build-url] [][npm-url] [][downloads-url] [][issues-url] [][codecov-url] [][semantic-release-url] [][commitizen-url] [](","source